  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Orange Pinwheel (Marasmius siccus) is a small, delicate fungus characterized by its distinctive orange-red coloring and radial gill arrangement that resembles a pinwheel. This tiny mushroom is a common wood decomposer found throughout New England's forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Radial Gill Pattern",
      "description": "Gills arranged in a distinctive pinwheel or spoke-like pattern radiating from the center, which is the most diagnostic feature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Orange-Red Cap Color",
      "description": "Small cap (typically 0.5-1 inch) with bright orange to reddish coloring that may fade with age or moisture",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin, Wiry Stem",
      "description": "Very thin, dark reddish-brown stem that is tough and fibrous rather than brittle",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Orange Pinwheels thrive on dead hardwood, leaf litter, and decaying wood debris on the forest floor throughout New England woodlands.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their delicate appearance, these mushrooms are incredibly tough and can revive from complete desiccation by absorbing moisture, allowing them to fruit repeatedly throughout the season.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
